description:
## Caller contract risk: two semantically-distinct catch-all fields with similar-looking names.

\`Mdn\` (RFC 9007) carries two adjacent fields that look like \"extension data\" to a casual reader but mean very different things on the wire:

\`\`\`rust
pub struct Mdn {
    // ...
    /// Extension fields from the MDN.
    ///
    /// Wire name is \`extensionFields\` per RFC 9007 §2 (normative).
    /// The §3.1 example incorrectly uses \`extension\`; the §2 definition is authoritative.
    #[serde(skip_serializing_if = \"Option::is_none\")]
    pub extension_fields: Option<HashMap<String, String>>,

    /// Catch-all for vendor / site / private extension fields not covered
    /// by the typed fields above. [...]
    /// Distinct from RFC 9007 \`extensionFields\` above: that field carries
    /// RFC 9007-defined MDN extension headers; \`extra\` captures JMAP-level
    /// vendor / site fields on the \`Mdn\` object itself.
    #[serde(flatten, default, skip_serializing_if = \"serde_json::Map::is_empty\")]
    pub extra: serde_json::Map<String, serde_json::Value>,
}
\`\`\`

The two fields are:

| Field | Wire shape | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| \`extension_fields\` (\`extensionFields\`) | inner object value | RFC 9007 §2 MDN-protocol extension headers — these go on the MIME-level MDN |
| \`extra\` (flattened) | sibling keys on the Mdn JSON object | Workspace vendor / site catch-all on the JMAP wire shape |

A caller who wants to attach a vendor MDN header (e.g. \`X-AcmeCorp-Tracking: abc123\`) reads the doc comment for \`extra\` (\"vendor / site / private extension fields\") and reasonably concludes that's where vendor extensions go. Wrong: that adds a key to the JMAP \`Mdn\` JSON object, which the server then has to special-case to translate into a MIME header — and most servers won't. The correct field is \`extension_fields\`.

A caller who wants to attach a vendor JMAP-level field (e.g. \`acmeCorpSendPriority: \"high\"\` for server-side dispatch hint) reads the doc comment for \`extension_fields\` (\"Extension fields from the MDN\") and reasonably concludes that's where extensions go. Wrong: that adds an MDN-protocol header field.

### Why the existing doc disambiguation is insufficient

The current doc on \`extra\` does include the cross-reference (\"Distinct from RFC 9007 \`extensionFields\` above...\"). But:

1. The disambiguation lives only on the \`extra\` side. A caller starting from \`extension_fields\` does not see it.
2. The two fields are listed back-to-back, which visually reinforces the \"both catch-alls\" reading.
3. Neither doc gives an example of when to use which.

### Two ways to harden

a. **Bilateral cross-reference**: add a \"See also: \[\`extra\`\] for JMAP-level vendor fields on this object\" line to the \`extension_fields\` docstring, mirroring the existing \"Distinct from RFC 9007 \`extensionFields\`\" line on \`extra\`.

b. **Worked example** in both docs: 1 line of Rust showing the right field for a representative caller (one vendor MDN header, one vendor JMAP field).

This is the only Mdn-family type where this collision exists, but the pattern (RFC-defined extension-headers field adjacent to workspace-policy \`extra\`) could recur in future extensions — flagging now sets a docs convention.
